    "final.course_reflection": "This activity made me think more about the connection between programming and practical robotics. Writing the movement pattern is not only about getting the Python syntax correct. I also had to think about coordinate frames, velocity, distance, turning radius, timing, and how the commands would affect a robot in the real world.\n\nOne thing that stood out to me was the difference between passing software tests and proving that something that works in the real world. My pattern could satisfy the mathematical and code requirements while live ROS requirements could still remain incomplete. That made me realize how important testing and safety are in robotics.\n\nThe activity also boosted my interest in robotics and computing because it combined programming with physical motion and problem solving. I liked being able to take a specification, translate it into code, create tests, find an error, fix it, and review the result.\n\nI saw how important it is in considering human and societal factors. A robot cannot operate alone, so safety, accessibility, pedestrians, and shared spaces matter in addition to whether the code fulfill all requirements. Overall, the activity showed me that good robotic programming requires both correct implementation and careful study of how a system behaves in the real world.\n",
    "final.synthesis": "A robot operation program can be well written and still fail as part of a social technical system because moving around the environment requires considerations of people, rules, and unexpected behavior. For example, a robot could be programmed to follow a programmed path through a shared hallway while still fulfilling its speed, turning, and stopping requirements, but the path could still create problems for pedestrians or block someone with accessibility needs. A mathematically correct trajectory does not automatically mean that the robot is safe, accessible, or convenient. Additional tests would be needed, including live testing in the actual environment, observations of pedestrian interactions, accessibility considerations, and confirmation that the robot can tolerate workplace or shared-space rules. This shows why technical tests should be compared with real-world evidence when evaluating autonomous or semi-autonomous robotic systems.\n",
